--TEST--
`Core\Process\Result::stdout` and `::stderr` are the child's two streams kept apart — one program writes a different marker to each, and each member is asserted to carry its own **and not the other's** — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members have the same signature, the same return type and adjacent
// slots, so a body reading the wrong one is a one-character defect that no
// "stdout is not empty" assertion can see. Both halves of each answer are named
// here for that reason: the marker that must be there, and the other stream's
// marker that must not. A member that answered its neighbour fails on the
// second half; one that answered the two streams merged — which is what a
// `2>&1` capture would hand back — fails on it too, while still looking right
// on the first.
//
// The markers are compared as `bytes`, never converted, because the octets a
// child writes are not this program's to assume anything about (`rule:types/bytes`, and
// this module's *Captured output is `bytes`, never `string`*). Containment
// rather than equality: each of the two shells below ends a line its own way
// and one of them pads before a redirect, and none of that is what this case is
// about.
//
// The child is the platform's own shell, for the reason
// `process-a-completed-run-answers-the-status-the-child-chose.nvst` states —
// § 1 makes a `PATH` lookup the caller's own, so each candidate is named
// absolutely, and the one that is not this platform's starts nothing —
// `IOError` on Windows, `RuntimeError` for a path with no root on Unix.

bytes $out = "" as bytes;
bytes $err = "" as bytes;
int $started = 0;

try {
    var $unix = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", "printf OUTPUT; printf DIAGNOSTIC 1>&2"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $out = $unix->stdout();
    $err = $unix->stderr();
} catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
}

try {
    var $windows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "echo OUTPUT& echo DIAGNOSTIC 1>&2"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $out = $windows->stdout();
    $err = $windows->stderr();
} catch (RuntimeError $notWindows) {
}

echo "started=", $started, "\n";

if (Core\Bytes::contains($out, "OUTPUT" as bytes)) {
    echo "stdout carries what the child printed\n";
} else {
    echo "stdout lost it\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($out, "DIAGNOSTIC" as bytes)) {
    echo "stdout also carries the other stream\n";
} else {
    echo "stdout carries nothing of the other stream\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($err, "DIAGNOSTIC" as bytes)) {
    echo "stderr carries what the child diagnosed\n";
} else {
    echo "stderr lost it\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($err, "OUTPUT" as bytes)) {
    echo "stderr also carries the other stream\n";
} else {
    echo "stderr carries nothing of the other stream\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true` for the reason the exit-code case gives: the door's own scoping is
# pinned elsewhere, and this case is about which stream is which.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
started=1
stdout carries what the child printed
stdout carries nothing of the other stream
stderr carries what the child diagnosed
stderr carries nothing of the other stream
